00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
kosovo's former prime minister  and the frontrunner in sunday's  elections cast his ballot  early in the day with this message today  citizens of kosovo  are exercising their right to vote hopes  are high a lot of challenges ahead  but we are hopeful that we are going to  have a  high turnout and a great result  for democracy for state building  and for future development and progress  albin corti has promised to rebuild  kosovo  and its institutions kosovo is  struggling to gain international  recognition  since its declaration of independence  from serbia 13 years ago  it is one of the poorest regions in  europe and has been struggling  economically for decades  one in three people are unemployed and  the average person  earns about 11 dollars a day  kosovo's 1.8 million voters  will choose 120 legislators from  among 1 000 candidates courtes main  opponent  is a prime minister abu lahati from the  center right kosovo democratic league  in his short time in the office hoti has  struggled with the coronavirus pandemic  with numbers increasing particularly in  recent weeks  despite the continued spread of the  virus many of the candidates contesting  sunday's pools  have failed to adhere to social  distancing measures  and there are fears a high turnout could  spread the virus  even more nevertheless the message from  both albin courty who is former activist  and decedent  and abdullah khoty whose party has  dominated the political scene for many  years  was for people to turn up and vote  i'm calling on all the citizens that  have the right to vote to use this right  and to vote  today is their day today they will  express their free will  ninety percent of kosovars are ethnic  albanians  and since the region declared its  independence there have been u.s  and eu brokered attempts to mediate  between  kosovo and serbia those have failed and  if court evince  analysts predict that hopes of reaching  a settlement could  further diminish but relations with  serbia  and potential membership in western  bodies and organizations  like nato and the european union have  not featured highly on the agenda during  the campaign  instead the focus has been mainly on the  economy  the main challenge for whoever wins  these elections will be to regain the  people's trust in the government and its  institutions  and to do that it would seem the new  leader will have to find the solution to  kosovo's economic problems  ognizorich al-jazeera pristina
 

